WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.200
INTRO:
Bill Gates is likely the most well known billionaire

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.290
in the entire world.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.220
Everyone knows him as the child prodigy that
dropped out of Harvard and founded one of

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.690
the biggest companies in the world: Microsoft.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:17.910
Since then, he’s dominated the top of the
billionaires list, currently standing at $112

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:18.960
billion.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.369
And that’s after donating more than $50
billion to charity.

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.980
Clearly, founding Microsoft has worked out
extremely well for Bill, but Bill Gates wasn’t

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.580
the sole founder of Microsoft?

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.800
In fact, Microsoft was actually cofounded
by another child prodigy named Paul Allen.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.730
And this wasn’t just some honorary title
either like Apple’s cofounder Ronald Wayne.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.930
Paul was heavily involved in the company for
nearly a decade before he decided to part

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:44.930
ways.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:49.850
At the time, he owned roughly 25% of Microsoft
which at the peak would translate to $625

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:50.850
billion.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:56.180
Yet, most people have no idea that Microsoft
even had a cofounder or that he tragically

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.850
died a few years ago.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.719
So, here’s what happend to the forgotton
founder of Microsoft.

00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:08.820
PRE MICROSOFT:
Taking a look back, Paul Allen was born on

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.440
January 21, 1953 in Seattle Washington.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.970
He was born to a pretty academic family as
both of his parents were heavily involved

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:16.970
in libraries.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:21.009
In fact, his father worked as the associate
director of libraries at the University of

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:22.009
Washington.

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:24.820
Meanwhile, his mom was a librarian.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.640
Hearing this, I don’t think you’d be surprised
to hear that his parents put him in the best

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.290
school that they could afford: a private preparatory
school called Lakeside School.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:36.610
For perspective, annual tuition at this school
currently costs $38,000.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:40.780
Aside from enrolling Paul in a prestigious
private school, his parents would also consistently

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:46.090
take him to museums, galleries, and concerts
to expose him to as many interests as possible.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.619
But, ironically, his greatest interest wasn’t
any of these.

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:53.340
His greatest interest was messing with computers
with his friends from school: Bill Gates and

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.509
Kent Evans.

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:58.430
This trio would regularly use Lakeside’s
Teletype terminals to develop their programming

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.729
skills, and soon enough, they would actually
found a programming club.

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:05.540
The only problem though was that they didn’t
any access to computers.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.950
So, they made a deal with a local computer
company called Computer Center Corporation.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.660
They offered to find bugs in their software
in exchange for computer time.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.950
It didn’t long for them to go from this
to developing scheduling software for Lakeside.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.020
Despite spending all this time on his hobbies,
Paul never slipped up academically either.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:27.000
In fact, he got a perfect 1600 on the SAT
before being admitted into Washington State

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.220
University.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:32.330
Paul was actually 2 years older than Bill,
so Bill was still in Seattle when Paul went

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.629
off to college.

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:39.110
This made it easy for them to stay in close
contact and work on their first serious business

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.110
endeavor called Traf-O-Data.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.681
At the time, state and local governments would
often perform traffic surveys in order to

00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:45.900
make stop lights more efficient.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.530
To accomplish this, they would lay out rubber
hoses on the road, and every time a car passed

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:54.590
by, it would create air pulses within the
rubber hose which was recorded by a mechanical

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:55.780
counter.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.240
Paul and Bill’s vision with Traf-O-Data
was to digitalize this entire process.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:04.280
But they once again had a fundamental issue,
they didn’t have access to a computer.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.930
So, they partnered with a man named Paul Wennerg
who could build them a computer.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:12.470
But just as everything was starting to get
off the ground, the trio would be stopped

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.000
cold in their tracks.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.140
The state of Washington would develop their
own traffic processing program which they

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:18.740
gave out for free to local governments.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:23.560
So, this unfortunately completely eliminated
the need for Traf-O-Data.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.170
By this point, Paul had finished two years
of college, but when he was offered a job

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:31.580
as a programmer at Honeywell in Boston, he
dropped out and took the job.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.599
This was actually perfect timing because Bill
would go to Harvard at the same time which

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:36.849
is also in Boston.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:41.720
So, the duo was able to continue their friendship
in Boston, and it didn’t take long for them

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.180
to start their next business venture.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:51.310
MICROSOFT:
In 1975, Paul and Bill would begin Microsoft,

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.440
but the company was nothing like what we know
them as today.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.209
The company was actually focused on selling
a programming language interpreter in New

00:03:58.209 --> 00:03:59.209
Mexico.

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:03.189
One of their first clients/partners was an
up and coming company called Micro Instrumentation

00:04:03.189 --> 00:04:04.510
and Telemetry Systems.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:09.189
And given that this company was located in
Albuquerque, New Mexico, Paul and Bill decided

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:10.769
to base Microsoft there as well.

00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:14.459
It wasn’t till 4 years later that they would
move the headquarters to their home state

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:15.769
of Washington.

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:19.910
In the meantime, Microsoft would get some
international exposure as well.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:24.840
In August of 1977, they would form an agreement
with ASCII Magazine in Japan which allowed

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.000
them to open ASCII Microsoft.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Despite all this progress with the company,
it didn’t take long for Bill and Paul to

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.650
start developing some animosity towards each
other.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:36.590
It all started with Bill wanting more equity
than Paul.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.780
The argument from Bill was that he was still
in college and making no money while Paul

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.320
already had a well paying job.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.750
So, Bill was not only taking a bigger risk
with Microsoft, but he would likely be able

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.810
to spend more time on Microsoft.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.860
This argument made sense to Paul, so he agreed
on splitting the equity 60-40.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.600
But, it wasn’t long before Bill wanted even
more equity.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.820
Bill soon renegotiated the contract to be
a 64-36 split.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.300
Paul didn’t want to make a big deal about
this and he knew that both of them would be

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.419
super rich anyway if things worked out.

00:05:05.419 --> 00:05:10.280
So, he didn’t really fight back against
Bill, but over time, things just got even

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:11.280
worse.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.690
As Microsoft started to attract press attention,
Bill become the public face of Microsoft which

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.530
slowly ate away at Paul’s significance within
the company.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:23.080
The two were quickly growing apart and they
both knew it, but Paul says that he was “Too

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.520
angry and proud” to confront Bill about
it.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.000
Bill’s personality didn’t make such a
confrontation much easier either.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.780
According to Paul, Bill was usually an extremely
sore loser and he often had difficulty admitting

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:34.850
his shortfalls.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:39.390
For example, Paul stopped playing chess with
Bill very early on because Bill would often

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.460
sweep the pieces onto the floor in anger whenever
he lost.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.940
While such quirks were likely quite annoying,
none of this was enough for Paul to leave

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:46.940
the company.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:51.590
He was extremely optimistic about the future
of Microsoft, but all of this would change

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:55.490
when he found out that he couldn’t be nearly
as optimistic about his own life.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:06:00.210
In 1982, at age 29, Paul would be diagnosed
with Hodgkin’s lymphoma which is a type

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.600
of cancer that affects the immune system.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:07.110
Despite this scary diagnosis, Paul would stick
around at Microsoft, but his input into the

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:10.259
company was significantly less for obvious
reasons.

00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:13.479
However, Bill had a different take on the
situation.

00:06:13.479 --> 00:06:17.020
Instead of supporting his friend through this
difficult period, Bill would start complaining

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.710
about how Paul was becoming unproductive.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.040
And the breaking point was when Paul overheard
Bill and Steve Ballmer discussing how they

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.090
could reduce Paul’s equity.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:29.169
After hearing this, Paul would do the honorable
thing and resign from Microsoft in February

00:06:29.169 --> 00:06:30.790
of 1983.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:36.770
POST MICROSOFT:
Fortunately, Paul’s cancer would get significantly

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:41.350
better following his departure from Microsoft,
but his mental health was worse than ever

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:42.350
before.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.380
You see, Paul didn’t really want to leave
Microsoft.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:49.840
He wanted to be part of something big, and
he had already founded something big.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.500
But, because of Bill, he was unable to continue
that journey.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:57.810
It should be noted that Paul was still on
Microsoft’s board, but this wasn’t what

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.289
he wanted per se.

00:06:59.289 --> 00:07:04.120
So, he kind of just wallowed in his own despair
for a few years after leaving Microsoft, but

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.770
all of this would change when Microsoft went
public in 1986.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:12.830
At the end of the first trading day, Microsoft
would be valued at $780 million which gave

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.000
Paul a net worth of $195 million.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.860
Paul now had boat loads of money and a life
threatening disease that could return at any

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:20.860
time.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.830
So, I don’t think you’d be surprised to
hear that Paul decided to stop feeling sorry

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.889
for himself and started living it up while
he still could.

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:30.199
But before he did that, he wanted to start
off with a positive task.

00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:35.340
So, in 1986, he founded the Paul G. Allen
Family Foundation which was dedicated to advancing

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.380
health and human services.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.670
After this, however, Paul started splurging.

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:43.910
He of course bought all of the classic things
like mansions, yachts, private jets, and sports

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:44.910
teams.

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:47.810
But he also had several creative engulgences
as well.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:51.840
For example, Paul was a massive fan of music
and especially rock music.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.710
So, in 2000, he opened the Experience Music
Project which was a rock and roll museum that

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:59.240
was dedicated to his rock hero Jimi Hendrix.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.569
He also created his own band called The Underthinkers
where he sang and played guitar.

00:08:03.569 --> 00:08:07.930
In 2013, he launched a full album in which
he personally wrote or cowrote every song

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:09.070
in the album.

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:12.580
Aside from music, Paul also began collecting
really unique items.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.810
For example, he built up a collection of WWII
fighter planes.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:19.430
He also had an extensive art collection that
was filled with pieces that were worth tens

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:20.430
of millions each.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:23.979
Paul didn’t just spend all of his money
though, he also invested significant portions

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:24.979
of it.

00:08:24.979 --> 00:08:28.000
For example, he had stakes in America Online
and DreamWorks.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.250
His sports bets also ended up playing out
pretty well as he owned the Seattle Seahawks,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.860
the Portland Trail Blazers, and the Seattle
Sounders.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:39.849
The Seahawks would end up making it to the
super bowl 3 times and they even won in 2014.

00:08:39.849 --> 00:08:42.440
Not all of his investments went so smoothly
though.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:46.700
Paul definitely had his fair share of losses
which in his case were massive.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:50.620
One of his investments, Charter communications,
filed for bankruptcy while many of his other

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:55.080
pet projects including Asymetrix, Starwave,
Metricom, and Interval Research never lived

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.269
up to what he envisioned.

00:08:56.269 --> 00:09:00.100
Nonetheless, his stake in Microsoft and his
other successful investments were more than

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:05.610
enough to make up for these given that he
would end up with a net worth of $20 billion.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.710
PAUL ALLEN:
In terms of Paul’s relationship with Bill

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:14.360
Gates after the fall out, they did eventually
make amends and get back onto a talking basis.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.060
But, it was never quite the same.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:20.890
Paul stuck around on Microsoft’s board up
until 2000 at which point he left as Bill

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.850
Gates stepped down from being CEO.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:28.030
Unfortunately, Paul’s lymphoma would resurface
in 2009 and though he would get treatment,

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:31.060
he was no doubt get weaker and weaker.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:34.940
As Paul saw the end of his life approaching,
he decided to write a memoir called Idea Man

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.660
that told the story of Microsoft from his
perspective.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:41.060
In the book, he claims that it was actually
him who came up with a bunch of the ideas

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:43.300
that Bill often gets credit for.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:48.020
For example, Paul says that he who came up
with the name Microsoft, and that he created

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:50.880
the SoftCard, and that he was the one who
thought of targeting the personal computer

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:51.880
market.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:55.720
According to Paul, he was the one that came
up with all the ideas while Bill spent most

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.420
of his time running the business itself.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.550
Many critics have pointed out that the book
is filled with bitterness and that it’s

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:03.450
more of a rant than an inspiring memoir.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.440
But, none of that really matters though as
Paul’s lymphoma would return yet again in

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:12.640
2018, and it would take his life on October
15, 2018 at age 65.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:16.500
In terms of what will happen to his fortune,
Paul never got married and had no children,

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:19.270
so he doesn’t have any heirs who will takeover.

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:23.029
Paul’s goal was to simply donate all of
his money after his death and he wrote a detailed

00:10:23.029 --> 00:10:24.570
plain regarding how to do that.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:28.940
So, all that’s left is for his sister, Jody
Allen, to actually execute his final vision.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:33.190
While he was never able to be the leading
voice at Microsoft, hopefully, he will continue

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:36.329
to be the leading voice behind his fortune.

00:10:36.329 --> 00:10:39.110
Would you guys rather be rich and sick or
poor and healthy?

